{"track":"apab","num":"4.02","slug":"linear-approximation-and-l-h-pital-s-rule","title":"Linear Approximation and L'Hôpital's Rule","subtitle":"ClipSAT Printable Practice Suite (clipsat.org) • Topic 4.02 • 20 FRQs + 25 MCQs • Difficulty Progression • Cross-Lesson Synthesis","sections":[{"heading":"Part I — Free-Response Questions (FRQs): Foundational Fluency (1–7)","questions":[{"q":"Use linearization (the tangent line) of $f(x) = 7\\sqrt{x}$ at $x = 2$ to approximate $f(2 + \\frac{1}{10})$.","difficulty":"Foundational","question_type":"FRQ","target":"Tangent Line Approximation (Linearization)","answer":"Final Answer: $\\frac{287 \\sqrt{2}}{40}$. $L(x)=f(2)+f'(2)(x-2)$. Here $f(2)=7 \\sqrt{2}$, $f'(2)=\\frac{7 \\sqrt{2}}{4}$, so $L(2 + \\frac{1}{10})\\approx \\frac{287 \\sqrt{2}}{40}$.","explanation":"$L(x)=f(2)+f'(2)(x-2)$. Here $f(2)=7 \\sqrt{2}$, $f'(2)=\\frac{7 \\sqrt{2}}{4}$, so $L(2 + \\frac{1}{10})\\approx \\frac{287 \\sqrt{2}}{40}$."},{"q":"Use linearization (the tangent line) of $f(x) = 6\\sqrt{x}$ at $x = 2$ to approximate $f(2 - \\frac{1}{10})$.","difficulty":"Foundational","question_type":"FRQ","target":"L'Hôpital's Rule for 0/0 Forms","answer":"Final Answer: $\\frac{117 \\sqrt{2}}{20}$. $L(x)=f(2)+f'(2)(x-2)$. Here $f(2)=6 \\sqrt{2}$, $f'(2)=\\frac{3 \\sqrt{2}}{2}$, so $L(2 - \\frac{1}{10})\\approx \\frac{117 \\sqrt{2}}{20}$.","explanation":"$L(x)=f(2)+f'(2)(x-2)$. Here $f(2)=6 \\sqrt{2}$, $f'(2)=\\frac{3 \\sqrt{2}}{2}$, so $L(2 - \\frac{1}{10})\\approx \\frac{117 \\sqrt{2}}{20}$.","figure":{"type":"triangle","vertices":[[0,0],[12,0],[0,5]],"labels":["$C$","$B$","$A$"],"side_labels":["$12$","$13$","$5$"],"right_angle_at":0,"angle_marks":[{"vertex":1,"label":"$\\theta$"}],"fill":true,"width":220}},{"q":"Use linearization (the tangent line) of $f(x) = 6\\sqrt{x}$ at $x = 5$ to approximate $f(5 - \\frac{1}{10})$.","difficulty":"Foundational","question_type":"FRQ","target":"L'Hôpital's Rule for Infinity/Infinity Forms","answer":"Final Answer: $\\frac{297 \\sqrt{5}}{50}$. $L(x)=f(5)+f'(5)(x-5)$. Here $f(5)=6 \\sqrt{5}$, $f'(5)=\\frac{3 \\sqrt{5}}{5}$, so $L(5 - \\frac{1}{10})\\approx \\frac{297 \\sqrt{5}}{50}$.","explanation":"$L(x)=f(5)+f'(5)(x-5)$. Here $f(5)=6 \\sqrt{5}$, $f'(5)=\\frac{3 \\sqrt{5}}{5}$, so $L(5 - \\frac{1}{10})\\approx \\frac{297 \\sqrt{5}}{50}$."},{"q":"Use linearization (the tangent line) of $f(x) = 7\\sqrt{x}$ at $x = 5$ to approximate $f(5 - \\frac{1}{5})$.","difficulty":"Foundational","question_type":"FRQ","target":"Comparing Relative Growth Rates of Functions","answer":"Final Answer: $\\frac{343 \\sqrt{5}}{50}$. $L(x)=f(5)+f'(5)(x-5)$. Here $f(5)=7 \\sqrt{5}$, $f'(5)=\\frac{7 \\sqrt{5}}{10}$, so $L(5 - \\frac{1}{5})\\approx \\frac{343 \\sqrt{5}}{50}$.","explanation":"$L(x)=f(5)+f'(5)(x-5)$. Here $f(5)=7 \\sqrt{5}$, $f'(5)=\\frac{7 \\sqrt{5}}{10}$, so $L(5 - \\frac{1}{5})\\approx \\frac{343 \\sqrt{5}}{50}$."},{"q":"Use linearization (the tangent line) of $f(x) = 7\\sqrt{x}$ at $x = 2$ to approximate $f(2 - \\frac{1}{10})$.","difficulty":"Foundational","question_type":"FRQ","target":"Tangent Line Approximation (Linearization)","answer":"Final Answer: $\\frac{273 \\sqrt{2}}{40}$. $L(x)=f(2)+f'(2)(x-2)$. Here $f(2)=7 \\sqrt{2}$, $f'(2)=\\frac{7 \\sqrt{2}}{4}$, so $L(2 - \\frac{1}{10})\\approx \\frac{273 \\sqrt{2}}{40}$.","explanation":"$L(x)=f(2)+f'(2)(x-2)$. Here $f(2)=7 \\sqrt{2}$, $f'(2)=\\frac{7 \\sqrt{2}}{4}$, so $L(2 - \\frac{1}{10})\\approx \\frac{273 \\sqrt{2}}{40}$."},{"q":"Use linearization (the tangent line) of $f(x) = 3\\sqrt{x}$ at $x = 5$ to approximate $f(5 + \\frac{1}{5})$.","difficulty":"Foundational","question_type":"FRQ","target":"L'Hôpital's Rule for 0/0 Forms","answer":"Final Answer: $\\frac{153 \\sqrt{5}}{50}$. $L(x)=f(5)+f'(5)(x-5)$. Here $f(5)=3 \\sqrt{5}$, $f'(5)=\\frac{3 \\sqrt{5}}{10}$, so $L(5 + \\frac{1}{5})\\approx \\frac{153 \\sqrt{5}}{50}$.","explanation":"$L(x)=f(5)+f'(5)(x-5)$. Here $f(5)=3 \\sqrt{5}$, $f'(5)=\\frac{3 \\sqrt{5}}{10}$, so $L(5 + \\frac{1}{5})\\approx \\frac{153 \\sqrt{5}}{50}$.","figure":{"type":"triangle","vertices":[[0,0],[8,0],[0,6]],"labels":["$C$","$B$","$A$"],"side_labels":["$8$","$10$","$6$"],"right_angle_at":0,"angle_marks":[{"vertex":1,"label":"$\\theta$"}],"fill":true,"width":220}},{"q":"Use linearization (the tangent line) of $f(x) = 6\\sqrt{x}$ at $x = 5$ to approximate $f(5 + \\frac{1}{5})$.","difficulty":"Foundational","question_type":"FRQ","target":"L'Hôpital's Rule for Infinity/Infinity Forms","answer":"Final Answer: $\\frac{153 \\sqrt{5}}{25}$. $L(x)=f(5)+f'(5)(x-5)$. Here $f(5)=6 \\sqrt{5}$, $f'(5)=\\frac{3 \\sqrt{5}}{5}$, so $L(5 + \\frac{1}{5})\\approx \\frac{153 \\sqrt{5}}{25}$.","explanation":"$L(x)=f(5)+f'(5)(x-5)$. Here $f(5)=6 \\sqrt{5}$, $f'(5)=\\frac{3 \\sqrt{5}}{5}$, so $L(5 + \\frac{1}{5})\\approx \\frac{153 \\sqrt{5}}{25}$."}]},{"heading":"Part II — Free-Response Questions (FRQs): Intermediate Multi-Step Application (8–14)","questions":[{"q":"Use linearization (the tangent line) of $f(x) = 5\\sqrt{x}$ at $x = 3$ to approximate $f(3 + \\frac{1}{10})$.","difficulty":"Intermediate","question_type":"FRQ","target":"Comparing Relative Growth Rates of Functions","answer":"Final Answer: $\\frac{61 \\sqrt{3}}{12}$. $L(x)=f(3)+f'(3)(x-3)$. Here $f(3)=5 \\sqrt{3}$, $f'(3)=\\frac{5 \\sqrt{3}}{6}$, so $L(3 + \\frac{1}{10})\\approx \\frac{61 \\sqrt{3}}{12}$.","explanation":"$L(x)=f(3)+f'(3)(x-3)$. Here $f(3)=5 \\sqrt{3}$, $f'(3)=\\frac{5 \\sqrt{3}}{6}$, so $L(3 + \\frac{1}{10})\\approx \\frac{61 \\sqrt{3}}{12}$."},{"q":"Use linearization (the tangent line) of $f(x) = 7\\sqrt{x}$ at $x = 3$ to approximate $f(3 - \\frac{1}{5})$.","difficulty":"Intermediate","question_type":"FRQ","target":"Tangent Line Approximation (Linearization)","answer":"Final Answer: $\\frac{203 \\sqrt{3}}{30}$. $L(x)=f(3)+f'(3)(x-3)$. Here $f(3)=7 \\sqrt{3}$, $f'(3)=\\frac{7 \\sqrt{3}}{6}$, so $L(3 - \\frac{1}{5})\\approx \\frac{203 \\sqrt{3}}{30}$.","explanation":"$L(x)=f(3)+f'(3)(x-3)$. Here $f(3)=7 \\sqrt{3}$, $f'(3)=\\frac{7 \\sqrt{3}}{6}$, so $L(3 - \\frac{1}{5})\\approx \\frac{203 \\sqrt{3}}{30}$."},{"q":"Use linearization (the tangent line) of $f(x) = 5\\sqrt{x}$ at $x = 2$ to approximate $f(2 + \\frac{1}{10})$.","difficulty":"Intermediate","question_type":"FRQ","target":"L'Hôpital's Rule for 0/0 Forms","answer":"Final Answer: $\\frac{41 \\sqrt{2}}{8}$. $L(x)=f(2)+f'(2)(x-2)$. Here $f(2)=5 \\sqrt{2}$, $f'(2)=\\frac{5 \\sqrt{2}}{4}$, so $L(2 + \\frac{1}{10})\\approx \\frac{41 \\sqrt{2}}{8}$.","explanation":"$L(x)=f(2)+f'(2)(x-2)$. Here $f(2)=5 \\sqrt{2}$, $f'(2)=\\frac{5 \\sqrt{2}}{4}$, so $L(2 + \\frac{1}{10})\\approx \\frac{41 \\sqrt{2}}{8}$.","figure":{"type":"triangle","vertices":[[0,0],[24,0],[0,7]],"labels":["$C$","$B$","$A$"],"side_labels":["$24$","$25$","$7$"],"right_angle_at":0,"angle_marks":[{"vertex":1,"label":"$\\theta$"}],"fill":true,"width":220}},{"q":"Use linearization (the tangent line) of $f(x) = 4\\sqrt{x}$ at $x = 3$ to approximate $f(3 + \\frac{1}{5})$.","difficulty":"Intermediate","question_type":"FRQ","target":"L'Hôpital's Rule for Infinity/Infinity Forms","answer":"Final Answer: $\\frac{62 \\sqrt{3}}{15}$. $L(x)=f(3)+f'(3)(x-3)$. Here $f(3)=4 \\sqrt{3}$, $f'(3)=\\frac{2 \\sqrt{3}}{3}$, so $L(3 + \\frac{1}{5})\\approx \\frac{62 \\sqrt{3}}{15}$.","explanation":"$L(x)=f(3)+f'(3)(x-3)$. Here $f(3)=4 \\sqrt{3}$, $f'(3)=\\frac{2 \\sqrt{3}}{3}$, so $L(3 + \\frac{1}{5})\\approx \\frac{62 \\sqrt{3}}{15}$."},{"q":"Use linearization (the tangent line) of $f(x) = 2\\sqrt{x}$ at $x = 2$ to approximate $f(2 + \\frac{1}{10})$.","difficulty":"Intermediate","question_type":"FRQ","target":"Comparing Relative Growth Rates of Functions","answer":"Final Answer: $\\frac{41 \\sqrt{2}}{20}$. $L(x)=f(2)+f'(2)(x-2)$. Here $f(2)=2 \\sqrt{2}$, $f'(2)=\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}$, so $L(2 + \\frac{1}{10})\\approx \\frac{41 \\sqrt{2}}{20}$.","explanation":"$L(x)=f(2)+f'(2)(x-2)$. Here $f(2)=2 \\sqrt{2}$, $f'(2)=\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}$, so $L(2 + \\frac{1}{10})\\approx \\frac{41 \\sqrt{2}}{20}$."},{"q":"Use linearization (the tangent line) of $f(x) = 2\\sqrt{x}$ at $x = 3$ to approximate $f(3 - \\frac{1}{10})$.","difficulty":"Intermediate","question_type":"FRQ","target":"Tangent Line Approximation (Linearization)","answer":"Final Answer: $\\frac{59 \\sqrt{3}}{30}$. $L(x)=f(3)+f'(3)(x-3)$. Here $f(3)=2 \\sqrt{3}$, $f'(3)=\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3}$, so $L(3 - \\frac{1}{10})\\approx \\frac{59 \\sqrt{3}}{30}$.","explanation":"$L(x)=f(3)+f'(3)(x-3)$. Here $f(3)=2 \\sqrt{3}$, $f'(3)=\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3}$, so $L(3 - \\frac{1}{10})\\approx \\frac{59 \\sqrt{3}}{30}$."},{"q":"Use linearization (the tangent line) of $f(x) = 5\\sqrt{x}$ at $x = 2$ to approximate $f(2 + \\frac{1}{5})$.","difficulty":"Intermediate","question_type":"FRQ","target":"L'Hôpital's Rule for 0/0 Forms","answer":"Final Answer: $\\frac{21 \\sqrt{2}}{4}$. $L(x)=f(2)+f'(2)(x-2)$. Here $f(2)=5 \\sqrt{2}$, $f'(2)=\\frac{5 \\sqrt{2}}{4}$, so $L(2 + \\frac{1}{5})\\approx \\frac{21 \\sqrt{2}}{4}$.","explanation":"$L(x)=f(2)+f'(2)(x-2)$. Here $f(2)=5 \\sqrt{2}$, $f'(2)=\\frac{5 \\sqrt{2}}{4}$, so $L(2 + \\frac{1}{5})\\approx \\frac{21 \\sqrt{2}}{4}$."}]},{"heading":"Part III — Free-Response Questions (FRQs): Challenging & Cross-Lesson Synthesis (15–20)","questions":[{"q":"Use linearization (the tangent line) of $f(x) = 2\\sqrt{x}$ at $x = 2$ to approximate $f(2 - \\frac{1}{10})$.","difficulty":"Challenging","question_type":"FRQ","target":"L'Hôpital's Rule for Infinity/Infinity Forms","cross_lesson":"5.01 Extrema and the Mean Value Theorem","answer":"Final Answer: $\\frac{39 \\sqrt{2}}{20}$. $L(x)=f(2)+f'(2)(x-2)$. Here $f(2)=2 \\sqrt{2}$, $f'(2)=\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}$, so $L(2 - \\frac{1}{10})\\approx \\frac{39 \\sqrt{2}}{20}$.","explanation":"$L(x)=f(2)+f'(2)(x-2)$. Here $f(2)=2 \\sqrt{2}$, $f'(2)=\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}$, so $L(2 - \\frac{1}{10})\\approx \\frac{39 \\sqrt{2}}{20}$."},{"q":"Use linearization (the tangent line) of $f(x) = 2\\sqrt{x}$ at $x = 5$ to approximate $f(5 - \\frac{1}{10})$.","difficulty":"Challenging","question_type":"FRQ","target":"Comparing Relative Growth Rates of Functions","cross_lesson":"5.02 Concavity, Curve Sketching, and Optimization","answer":"Final Answer: $\\frac{99 \\sqrt{5}}{50}$. $L(x)=f(5)+f'(5)(x-5)$. Here $f(5)=2 \\sqrt{5}$, $f'(5)=\\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{5}$, so $L(5 - \\frac{1}{10})\\approx \\frac{99 \\sqrt{5}}{50}$.","explanation":"$L(x)=f(5)+f'(5)(x-5)$. Here $f(5)=2 \\sqrt{5}$, $f'(5)=\\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{5}$, so $L(5 - \\frac{1}{10})\\approx \\frac{99 \\sqrt{5}}{50}$.","figure":{"type":"triangle","vertices":[[0,0],[8,0],[0,6]],"labels":["$C$","$B$","$A$"],"side_labels":["$8$","$10$","$6$"],"right_angle_at":0,"angle_marks":[{"vertex":1,"label":"$\\theta$"}],"fill":true,"width":220}},{"q":"Use linearization (the tangent line) of $f(x) = 5\\sqrt{x}$ at $x = 5$ to approximate $f(5 - \\frac{1}{10})$.","difficulty":"Challenging","question_type":"FRQ","target":"Tangent Line Approximation (Linearization)","cross_lesson":"6.01 Antiderivatives and Riemann Sums","answer":"Final Answer: $\\frac{99 \\sqrt{5}}{20}$. $L(x)=f(5)+f'(5)(x-5)$. Here $f(5)=5 \\sqrt{5}$, $f'(5)=\\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{2}$, so $L(5 - \\frac{1}{10})\\approx \\frac{99 \\sqrt{5}}{20}$.","explanation":"$L(x)=f(5)+f'(5)(x-5)$. Here $f(5)=5 \\sqrt{5}$, $f'(5)=\\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{2}$, so $L(5 - \\frac{1}{10})\\approx \\frac{99 \\sqrt{5}}{20}$."},{"q":"Use linearization (the tangent line) of $f(x) = 3\\sqrt{x}$ at $x = 3$ to approximate $f(3 + \\frac{1}{5})$.","difficulty":"Challenging","question_type":"FRQ","target":"L'Hôpital's Rule for 0/0 Forms","cross_lesson":"6.02 The Fundamental Theorem of Calculus","answer":"Final Answer: $\\frac{31 \\sqrt{3}}{10}$. $L(x)=f(3)+f'(3)(x-3)$. Here $f(3)=3 \\sqrt{3}$, $f'(3)=\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}$, so $L(3 + \\frac{1}{5})\\approx \\frac{31 \\sqrt{3}}{10}$.","explanation":"$L(x)=f(3)+f'(3)(x-3)$. Here $f(3)=3 \\sqrt{3}$, $f'(3)=\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}$, so $L(3 + \\frac{1}{5})\\approx \\frac{31 \\sqrt{3}}{10}$."},{"q":"Use linearization (the tangent line) of $f(x) = 2\\sqrt{x}$ at $x = 5$ to approximate $f(5 - \\frac{1}{5})$.","difficulty":"Challenging","question_type":"FRQ","target":"L'Hôpital's Rule for Infinity/Infinity Forms","cross_lesson":"7.01 Differential Equations and Slope Fields","answer":"Final Answer: $\\frac{49 \\sqrt{5}}{25}$. $L(x)=f(5)+f'(5)(x-5)$. Here $f(5)=2 \\sqrt{5}$, $f'(5)=\\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{5}$, so $L(5 - \\frac{1}{5})\\approx \\frac{49 \\sqrt{5}}{25}$.","explanation":"$L(x)=f(5)+f'(5)(x-5)$. Here $f(5)=2 \\sqrt{5}$, $f'(5)=\\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{5}$, so $L(5 - \\frac{1}{5})\\approx \\frac{49 \\sqrt{5}}{25}$."},{"q":"Use linearization (the tangent line) of $f(x) = 2\\sqrt{x}$ at $x = 2$ to approximate $f(2 + \\frac{1}{5})$.","difficulty":"Challenging","question_type":"FRQ","target":"Comparing Relative Growth Rates of Functions","cross_lesson":"8.01 Area Between Curves","answer":"Final Answer: $\\frac{21 \\sqrt{2}}{10}$. $L(x)=f(2)+f'(2)(x-2)$. Here $f(2)=2 \\sqrt{2}$, $f'(2)=\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}$, so $L(2 + \\frac{1}{5})\\approx \\frac{21 \\sqrt{2}}{10}$.","explanation":"$L(x)=f(2)+f'(2)(x-2)$. Here $f(2)=2 \\sqrt{2}$, $f'(2)=\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}$, so $L(2 + \\frac{1}{5})\\approx \\frac{21 \\sqrt{2}}{10}$."}]},{"heading":"Part IV — Multiple-Choice Questions (MCQs): Foundational Concept Mastery (21–28)","questions":[{"q":"What is the local linear approximation $L(x)$ of $f(x) = \\sqrt{x}$ at $x = 25$?","difficulty":"Foundational","question_type":"MCQ","target":"Tangent Line Approximation (Linearization)","choices":["$L(x) = 5 + \\frac{1}{10}(x - 25)$","$L(x) = 5 + \\frac{1}{5}(x - 25)$","$L(x) = 5 + \\frac{1}{2}(x - 25)$","$L(x) = \\frac{1}{10}x$"],"correct":0,"answer":"$f(25) = 5$. $f'(x) = \\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{x}} \\Rightarrow f'(25) = \\frac{1}{10}$. Linearization: $L(x) = f(a) + f'(a)(x - a) = 5 + \\frac{1}{10}(x - 25)$.","explanation":"$f(25) = 5$. $f'(x) = \\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{x}} \\Rightarrow f'(25) = \\frac{1}{10}$. Linearization: $L(x) = f(a) + f'(a)(x - a) = 5 + \\frac{1}{10}(x - 25)$."},{"q":"Using the linearization of $f(x) = \\sqrt{x}$ at $a = 25$, approximate $\\sqrt{26}$.","difficulty":"Foundational","question_type":"MCQ","target":"Tangent Line Approximation (Linearization)","choices":["$5.10$","$5.05$","$5.20$","$5.01$"],"correct":0,"answer":"$L(26) = 5 + 0.1(26 - 25) = 5 + 0.1 = 5.10$.","explanation":"$L(26) = 5 + 0.1(26 - 25) = 5 + 0.1 = 5.10$."},{"q":"Is the tangent line approximation for $f(x) = \\sqrt{x}$ an overestimate or underestimate, and why?","difficulty":"Foundational","question_type":"MCQ","target":"Tangent Line Approximation (Linearization)","choices":["An overestimate because $f''(x) < 0$ (concave down)","An underestimate because $f''(x) > 0$ (concave up)","Exact because $f$ is monotonic","Depends on the step size"],"correct":0,"answer":"Since $f''(x) = -\\frac{1}{4} x^{-3/2} < 0$, the curve is concave down and lies below its tangent lines, making tangent line approximations overestimates.","explanation":"Since $f''(x) = -\\frac{1}{4} x^{-3/2} < 0$, the curve is concave down and lies below its tangent lines, making tangent line approximations overestimates.","figure":{"type":"triangle","vertices":[[0,0],[15,0],[0,8]],"labels":["$C$","$B$","$A$"],"side_labels":["$15$","$17$","$8$"],"right_angle_at":0,"angle_marks":[{"vertex":1,"label":"$\\theta$"}],"fill":true,"width":220}},{"q":"Evaluate $\\lim_{x \\rightarrow 0} \\frac{e^{2x} - 1}{\\sin(3x)}$.","difficulty":"Foundational","question_type":"MCQ","target":"L'Hôpital's Rule for 0/0 Forms","choices":["$\\frac{2}{3}$","$\\frac{3}{2}$","$0$","$\\infty$"],"correct":0,"answer":"Direct substitution yields $\\frac{0}{0}$. By L'Hôpital's Rule: $\\lim_{x \\rightarrow 0} \\frac{2e^{2x}}{3\\cos(3x)} = \\frac{2(1)}{3(1)} = \\frac{2}{3}$.","explanation":"Direct substitution yields $\\frac{0}{0}$. By L'Hôpital's Rule: $\\lim_{x \\rightarrow 0} \\frac{2e^{2x}}{3\\cos(3x)} = \\frac{2(1)}{3(1)} = \\frac{2}{3}$."},{"q":"Evaluate $\\lim_{x \\rightarrow 1} \\frac{\\ln x}{x^2 - 1}$.","difficulty":"Foundational","question_type":"MCQ","target":"L'Hôpital's Rule for 0/0 Forms","choices":["$\\frac{1}{2}$","$1$","$2$","$0$"],"correct":0,"answer":"$\\frac{0}{0}$ form. By L'Hôpital's: $\\lim_{x \\rightarrow 1} \\frac{1/x}{2x} = \\frac{1}{2(1)} = \\frac{1}{2}$.","explanation":"$\\frac{0}{0}$ form. By L'Hôpital's: $\\lim_{x \\rightarrow 1} \\frac{1/x}{2x} = \\frac{1}{2(1)} = \\frac{1}{2}$."},{"q":"Evaluate $\\lim_{x \\rightarrow 0} \\frac{1 - \\cos(4x)}{x^2}$.","difficulty":"Foundational","question_type":"MCQ","target":"L'Hôpital's Rule for 0/0 Forms","choices":["$8$","$4$","$2$","$16$"],"correct":0,"answer":"First L'Hôpital's: $\\frac{4\\sin(4x)}{2x}$. Second L'Hôpital's: $\\frac{16\\cos(4x)}{2} = \\frac{16(1)}{2} = 8$.","explanation":"First L'Hôpital's: $\\frac{4\\sin(4x)}{2x}$. Second L'Hôpital's: $\\frac{16\\cos(4x)}{2} = \\frac{16(1)}{2} = 8$."},{"q":"Evaluate $\\lim_{x \\rightarrow \\infty} \\frac{\\ln(x)}{x}$.","difficulty":"Foundational","question_type":"MCQ","target":"L'Hôpital's Rule for Infinity/Infinity Forms","choices":["$0$","$1$","$\\infty$","Undefined"],"correct":0,"answer":"$\\frac{\\infty}{\\infty}$ form. By L'Hôpital's: $\\lim_{x \\rightarrow \\infty} \\frac{1/x}{1} = 0$.","explanation":"$\\frac{\\infty}{\\infty}$ form. By L'Hôpital's: $\\lim_{x \\rightarrow \\infty} \\frac{1/x}{1} = 0$."},{"q":"Evaluate $\\lim_{x \\rightarrow \\infty} \\frac{x^2}{e^x}$.","difficulty":"Foundational","question_type":"MCQ","target":"L'Hôpital's Rule for Infinity/Infinity Forms","choices":["$0$","$1$","$2$","$\\infty$"],"correct":0,"answer":"Apply L'Hôpital's twice: $\\lim_{x \\rightarrow \\infty} \\frac{2x}{e^x} = \\lim_{x \\rightarrow \\infty} \\frac{2}{e^x} = 0$.","explanation":"Apply L'Hôpital's twice: $\\lim_{x \\rightarrow \\infty} \\frac{2x}{e^x} = \\lim_{x \\rightarrow \\infty} \\frac{2}{e^x} = 0$.","figure":{"type":"plot","fns":["1 + 1.5/(x - (-1))"],"xmin":-5,"xmax":5,"ymin":-4,"ymax":6,"asymptotes":{"x":[-1],"y":[1]},"point_labels":[{"x":0.5,"y":2,"label":"$( 0.5, 2 )$"}],"width":230}}]},{"heading":"Part V — Multiple-Choice Questions (MCQs): Intermediate Exam Application (29–37)","questions":[{"q":"Which requirement MUST be verified before applying L'Hôpital's Rule to $\\lim_{x \\rightarrow c} \\frac{f(x)}{g(x)}$?","difficulty":"Intermediate","question_type":"MCQ","target":"L'Hôpital's Rule for Infinity/Infinity Forms","choices":["The limit produces an indeterminate form $\\frac{0}{0}$ or $\\frac{\\pm\\infty}{\\pm\\infty}$","$f(c) = 0$ only","$f'(c) = 0$","$g(x) > 0$ for all $x$"],"correct":0,"answer":"L'Hôpital's Rule applies ONLY when the quotient evaluates to an indeterminate form of type $\\frac{0}{0}$ or $\\frac{\\pm\\infty}{\\pm\\infty}$.","explanation":"L'Hôpital's Rule applies ONLY when the quotient evaluates to an indeterminate form of type $\\frac{0}{0}$ or $\\frac{\\pm\\infty}{\\pm\\infty}$."},{"q":"As $x \\rightarrow \\infty$, which of the following functions grows the fastest?","difficulty":"Intermediate","question_type":"MCQ","target":"Comparing Relative Growth Rates of Functions","choices":["$e^x$","$x^{10}$","$x \\ln x$","$2^x$"],"correct":0,"answer":"Exponentials with larger base grow faster than any polynomial or power function. Since $e \\approx 2.718 > 2$, $e^x$ grows fastest.","explanation":"Exponentials with larger base grow faster than any polynomial or power function. Since $e \\approx 2.718 > 2$, $e^x$ grows fastest."},{"q":"Evaluate $\\lim_{x \\rightarrow \\infty} \\frac{(\\ln x)^5}{\\sqrt{x}}$.","difficulty":"Intermediate","question_type":"MCQ","target":"Comparing Relative Growth Rates of Functions","choices":["$0$","$\\infty$","$1$","$5$"],"correct":0,"answer":"Any power of $x$ (even $x^{1/2}$) grows strictly faster than any power of $\\ln x$. Hence the limit is $0$.","explanation":"Any power of $x$ (even $x^{1/2}$) grows strictly faster than any power of $\\ln x$. Hence the limit is $0$."},{"q":"If $\\lim_{x \\rightarrow \\infty} \\frac{f(x)}{g(x)} = \\infty$, what does this conclude about their growth rates?","difficulty":"Intermediate","question_type":"MCQ","target":"Comparing Relative Growth Rates of Functions","choices":["$f(x)$ grows faster than $g(x)$","$g(x)$ grows faster than $f(x)$","They grow at the same rate","Neither function grows"],"correct":0,"answer":"By definition of relative growth rates, $\\lim_{x \\rightarrow \\infty} \\frac{f(x)}{g(x)} = \\infty$ indicates that $f(x)$ grows strictly faster than $g(x)$.","explanation":"By definition of relative growth rates, $\\lim_{x \\rightarrow \\infty} \\frac{f(x)}{g(x)} = \\infty$ indicates that $f(x)$ grows strictly faster than $g(x)$."},{"q":"Use linearization (the tangent line) of $f(x) = 1\\sqrt{x}$ at $x = 2$ to approximate $f(2 - \\frac{1}{10})$.","difficulty":"Intermediate","question_type":"MCQ","target":"Tangent Line Approximation (Linearization)","choices":["$-1 + \\frac{39 \\sqrt{2}}{40}$","$\\sqrt{2}$","$1 + \\frac{39 \\sqrt{2}}{40}$","$\\frac{39 \\sqrt{2}}{40}$"],"correct":3,"answer":"$L(x)=f(2)+f'(2)(x-2)$. Here $f(2)=\\sqrt{2}$, $f'(2)=\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{4}$, so $L(2 - \\frac{1}{10})\\approx \\frac{39 \\sqrt{2}}{40}$.","explanation":"$L(x)=f(2)+f'(2)(x-2)$. Here $f(2)=\\sqrt{2}$, $f'(2)=\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{4}$, so $L(2 - \\frac{1}{10})\\approx \\frac{39 \\sqrt{2}}{40}$.","figure":{"type":"triangle","vertices":[[0,0],[15,0],[0,8]],"labels":["$C$","$B$","$A$"],"side_labels":["$15$","$17$","$8$"],"right_angle_at":0,"angle_marks":[{"vertex":1,"label":"$\\theta$"}],"fill":true,"width":220}},{"q":"Use linearization (the tangent line) of $f(x) = 1\\sqrt{x}$ at $x = 3$ to approximate $f(3 + \\frac{1}{10})$.","difficulty":"Intermediate","question_type":"MCQ","target":"L'Hôpital's Rule for 0/0 Forms","choices":["$\\sqrt{3}$","$\\frac{61 \\sqrt{3}}{60}$","$1 + \\frac{61 \\sqrt{3}}{60}$","$-1 + \\frac{61 \\sqrt{3}}{60}$"],"correct":1,"answer":"$L(x)=f(3)+f'(3)(x-3)$. Here $f(3)=\\sqrt{3}$, $f'(3)=\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{6}$, so $L(3 + \\frac{1}{10})\\approx \\frac{61 \\sqrt{3}}{60}$.","explanation":"$L(x)=f(3)